# Wealthee Firebase Function Calls

## Overview

The `callFirebaseFunction` utility provides standardized error handling, authentication, retry capability, and monitoring when interacting with Firebase Cloud Functions. **Always use this utility instead of direct fetch calls** when communicating with Firebase Functions.

## Required Usage Pattern

```typescript
import { callFirebaseFunction } from "@/lib/firebase-functions";

// Typed response for better type safety
interface MyFunctionResponse {
    id: string;
    name: string;
}

// Correct implementation
async function fetchDataFromFirebase(userId: string): Promise<MyFunctionResponse> {
  const result = await callFirebaseFunction<MyFunctionResponse>({
    idToken: "user-auth-token", // From auth context
    functionName: "my-firebase-function-name",
    queryParams: { userId },
    options: {
      method: "GET",
      revalidate: 60, // Cache for 60 seconds
    }
  });

  return result;
}
```

## Parameters

| Parameter | Required | Description |
|-----------|----------|-------------|
| `idToken` | Optional | Firebase ID token for authenticated requests. Omit only for public functions. |
| `functionName` | Required | Name of the Firebase function to call (without domain/path). |
| `queryParams` | Optional | URL query parameters as a Record<string, string>. |
| `options` | Optional | Fetch configuration including headers, method, body, etc. |
| `retries` | Optional | Number of retry attempts for transient errors (default: 1). |

## Authentication Rules

1. **Always provide idToken for authenticated functions:**
   ```typescript
   // Correct - Authenticated request
   const result = await callFirebaseFunction({
     idToken: userToken, // From auth context
     functionName: "secured-function"
   });
   ```

2. **Only omit idToken for public functions:**
   ```typescript
   // Correct - Public function
   const result = await callFirebaseFunction({
     functionName: "public-health-check"
   });
   ```

## Error Handling

The utility provides built-in error handling, but you should still handle errors in your application code:

```typescript
try {
  const result = await callFirebaseFunction({
    functionName: "my-function",
    idToken
  });
  // Handle success
} catch (error) {
  // Check for specific error types
  if (error instanceof FirebaseFunctionError) {
    if (error.statusCode === 403) {
      // Handle permission error
    } else {
      // Handle other Firebase errors
    }
  } else {
    // Handle unexpected errors
  }
}
```

## Type Safety

Always provide a generic type parameter for proper type safety:

```typescript
// Correct - With type safety
const result = await callFirebaseFunction<UserData>({
  functionName: "get-user",
  idToken
});

// Incorrect - No type information
const result = await callFirebaseFunction({
  functionName: "get-user",
  idToken
});
```

## Common Mistakes to Avoid

1. **❌ DO NOT use direct fetch calls to Firebase Functions:**
   ```typescript
   // INCORRECT - direct fetch
   const response = await fetch(`https://us-central1-${projectId}.cloudfunctions.net/myFunction`);
   ```

2. **❌ DO NOT hardcode Firebase Function URLs:**
   ```typescript
   // INCORRECT - hardcoded URL
   const baseUrl = "https://my-function-abc123.cloudfunctions.net";
   ```

3. **❌ DO NOT implement your own retry logic or error handling:**
   ```typescript
   // INCORRECT - custom retry logic
   let attempts = 0;
   while (attempts < 3) {
     try {
       const response = await fetch(functionUrl);
       // ...
     } catch (error) {
       attempts++;
     }
   }
   ```

4. **❌ DO NOT pass sensitive data in query parameters:**
   ```typescript
   // INCORRECT - sensitive data in query params
   const result = await callFirebaseFunction({
     functionName: "update-user",
     queryParams: { password: "secret123" } // WRONG!
   });
   ```

## Best Practices

1. **Group related function calls in domain-specific service files:**
   ```typescript
   // services/users.ts
   export async function getUser(userId: string, idToken: string): Promise<User> {
     return callFirebaseFunction<User>({
       idToken,
       functionName: "get-user",
       queryParams: { userId }
     });
   }
   ```

2. **Use appropriate cache validation:**
   ```typescript
   // Cacheable data (GET requests)
   const data = await callFirebaseFunction({
     functionName: "get-static-data",
     options: { revalidate: 3600 } // Cache for 1 hour
   });

   // Real-time data (should not be cached)
   const data = await callFirebaseFunction({
     functionName: "get-real-time-data",
     options: { revalidate: 0 } // Don't cache
   });
   ```

3. **Handle function-specific errors at the service level:**
   ```typescript
   export async function deleteUser(userId: string, idToken: string) {
     try {
       return await callFirebaseFunction({
         idToken,
         functionName: "delete-user",
         queryParams: { userId },
         options: { method: "DELETE" }
       });
     } catch (error) {
       if (error instanceof FirebaseFunctionError && error.statusCode === 409) {
         throw new Error("User has active subscriptions and cannot be deleted");
       }
       throw error;
     }
   }
   ```

4. **Use appropriate HTTP methods:**
   ```typescript
   // GET - Retrieve data
   const user = await callFirebaseFunction({
     functionName: "get-user",
     queryParams: { userId },
     options: { method: "GET" }
   });

   // POST - Create resources
   const newUser = await callFirebaseFunction({
     functionName: "create-user",
     options: {
       method: "POST",
       body: JSON.stringify(userData)
     }
   });

   // PUT - Replace resources
   const updatedUser = await callFirebaseFunction({
     functionName: "update-user",
     options: {
       method: "PUT",
       body: JSON.stringify(userData)
     }
   });

   // DELETE - Remove resources
   const result = await callFirebaseFunction({
     functionName: "delete-user",
     queryParams: { userId },
     options: { method: "DELETE" }
   });
   ```

Remember: This utility centralizes error handling, authentication, and monitoring for all Firebase Function calls. Using it consistently ensures reliable communication with your backend.